      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x-4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3x+1}{x-4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3y+1}{y-4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3y+1}{y-4} \\\\ x(y-4) & =3y+1 \\\\ xy-4x & =3y+1 \\\\ xy-3y & =4x+1 \\\\ y(x-3) & =4x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+1}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4x+1}{x-3}.\n$$",
